WEBVTT

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:40.920
The polar winter.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:58.080
This is the planet at its most hostile.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:26.080
Those that stay here at this time must face the harshest conditions on Earth.

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:37.080
The high Arctic in late autumn, empty and abandoned to ice and snow.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:41.160
Most animals migrated south weeks ago.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:51.900
The few that stay will face violent winds, plunging temperatures and months of darkness.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:59.720
A female polar bear searches for shelter.

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:05.560
Other bears are out on the frozen ocean looking for food, but she won't feed again until next

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:10.760
spring.

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:23.280
Using as little energy as possible, she starts to dig a shallow nest.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:26.000
The snow here is easy to work.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:31.320
It's soft and light, a sure sign that soon plenty more will accumulate on this slope

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:35.600
as winter advances.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:48.120
And that is what she needs.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:03:07.080
If she has chosen well, the Arctic wind will do much of her work for her.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:12.240
Once the snow here is deep enough, she will dig down to make a den.

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:33.280
She'll then lie waiting for her cubs to be born as winter sets in.

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:37.880
The sea ice already covers twice the area it did in summer.

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:46.520
Those animals that a few weeks ago came down here to feed in the sea are now locked out.

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:57.720
But there are windows in this white desert, gateways to the rich ocean below.

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:03.520
Most birds have migrated south, but in the frozen Bering Sea, ducks of one particular

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:09.720
species are gathering together in a single immense flock.

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:28.320
Spectacled ides, hundreds of thousands of them.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:54.800
With the seas that fed them throughout the summer all but frozen, this is the Ida's

00:04:54.800 --> 00:05:04.120
last refuge.

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:13.800
Such patches of open water, kept free of ice by strong currents, are given a Russian name,

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:18.720
Polinyas.

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:25.800
If this Polinia stays open all winter, the ducks will have avoided an exhausting migration.

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:31.560
It's a gamble and a tremendous risk, especially for the spectacled Ida, for this Polinia holds

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:54.280
the world's entire population.

00:05:54.280 --> 00:06:02.000
Surrounded by ice, the spectacled Ida's survival depends on this single vulnerable oasis, and

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:08.840
conditions here can change very fast.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:20.720
A smaller pool.

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:24.760
Here common Ida ducks are learning a bitter lesson.

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:33.720
An early winter storm has caught them out, and the ice is closing in.

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:52.600
The pool is shrinking and the ducks are freezing to death.

00:06:52.600 --> 00:07:09.220
These gamblers have lost.

00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:26.160
As the sun's influence continues to dwindle here in the north, the cold pushes south into

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:35.280
Arctic lands.

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:42.800
Winter brings a devastatingly destructive force.

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:46.680
Frost.

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:52.680
Ice crystals form as moisture in the air freezes.

00:07:52.680 --> 00:08:01.680
Inside plants, the same thing is happening to the water in their cells and their sap.

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:15.720
With these first frosts, most plants die, their insides ripped apart by ice crystals.

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:24.400
Coniferous trees, however, like fir and pine, can withstand very low temperatures.

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:31.000
It's these trees that create the greatest forest on our planet.

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:40.120
This is the taiga.

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:56.840
It encircles the globe and contains one third of all the trees on Earth.

00:08:56.840 --> 00:09:03.280
As winter deepens, frigid air from the Arctic meets warm moist air from the south, producing

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:12.240
particularly heavy snows.

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:21.920
For the big animals of the forest, there's no way of hiding from the winter.

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:27.240
Its size for the bison of northern Canada is in fact their salvation.

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:32.760
Big bodies lose less heat and can carry more insulation.

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:41.300
That may be why these bison are the largest land animals in North America.

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:52.960
And only a large predator can tackle them.

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:57.640
Wolves hunt better in packs, but there are only two of them here and the prey they pursue

00:09:57.640 --> 00:10:16.080
are giants.

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:20.260
A vast Arctic wilderness stretches all around.

00:10:20.260 --> 00:10:29.800
In it, somewhere, there are bison.

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:34.120
These deep tracks in the snow are easy to follow.

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:51.740
But which way were the bison going?

00:10:51.740 --> 00:10:53.640
They have detected a scent.

00:10:53.640 --> 00:11:00.360
The hunt is on.

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:02.760
Running in the bison's tracks is easy.

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:07.080
The snow here is compressed and firm.

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:14.800
At this rate, the wolves will soon catch up.

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:18.320
They attempt a shortcut.

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:24.640
The giant bison can plow on through virgin snow, but for the wolves, deep snow is a hindrance

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:44.560
and now they're losing ground.

00:11:44.560 --> 00:12:07.040
The hunters rejoin the bison's tracks and the contest is on again.

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:10.060
The bison are the only prey here in winter.

00:12:10.060 --> 00:12:16.080
The wolves have no option, they must tackle them.

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:23.160
But bison are ten times their size.

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:42.880
In winter, the line between life and death is so narrow that for a wolf, even a small

00:12:42.880 --> 00:12:45.680
injury could be fatal.

00:12:45.680 --> 00:12:49.360
It's crucial they select the right target.

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:51.680
This one is very big.

00:12:51.680 --> 00:13:00.200
Good decision.

00:13:00.200 --> 00:13:19.160
This is smaller, a yearling.

00:13:19.160 --> 00:13:23.720
Even though it's young, it's nonetheless heavier than both wolves combined and it is

00:13:49.160 --> 00:13:58.400
The male wolf backs off, perhaps he's frightened of injury, but the female is more determined

00:13:58.400 --> 00:14:19.920
or more desperate.

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:45.860
Having given so much already, she must make the kill.

00:14:45.860 --> 00:15:01.080
This is a battle of life and death for them both.

00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:05.360
The bison has no strength left.

00:15:05.360 --> 00:15:19.440
The battle is over.

00:15:19.440 --> 00:15:21.400
Winter deepens.

00:15:21.400 --> 00:15:37.220
The snow continues to accumulate, smothering the forest.

00:15:37.220 --> 00:15:42.760
Some trees are loaded with three tons of snow.

00:15:42.760 --> 00:15:47.480
It shuts out what little light there is at these latitudes and that shortens the growing

00:15:47.480 --> 00:16:02.080
season still further and limits how far north trees are able to grow.

00:16:02.080 --> 00:16:14.880
So snow influences both the shapes of the trees and the extent of this forest.

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:20.000
And it affects the animals too.

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:27.040
It helps to have friends if you want to find food at this time of the year.

00:16:27.040 --> 00:16:33.400
Wolverines and ravens, a match made in the tiger.

00:16:33.400 --> 00:16:38.160
It's the hardest time of the year to find food, but the wolverine knows that a raven's

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:45.200
call is as good as a dinner bell.

00:16:45.200 --> 00:16:52.940
And the raven has just recruited the best can opener in the forest.

00:16:52.940 --> 00:16:58.200
The moose carcass is frozen solid, but the wolverine has immensely powerful jaws, well

00:16:58.200 --> 00:17:01.620
able to deal with frozen food.

00:17:01.620 --> 00:17:17.440
The raven's bill is more suited to leftovers.

00:17:17.440 --> 00:17:27.000
The raven has to be patient.

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:30.320
A wolverine's appetite is legendary.

00:17:30.320 --> 00:17:35.080
According to folklore, it can eat more at one sitting than any other creature in the

00:17:35.080 --> 00:17:50.560
forest, hence its other name, the glutton.

00:17:50.560 --> 00:17:55.880
What the wolverine can't eat now, it stores in the deep freeze.

00:17:55.880 --> 00:18:12.040
It will bury these food parcels across the forest, planning ahead for leaner times.

00:18:12.040 --> 00:18:32.280
For a few, the snow is an ally.

00:18:32.280 --> 00:18:42.920
Wolverines stay active throughout the winter beneath the snow, despite the freezing temperatures

00:18:42.920 --> 00:18:44.480
above.

00:18:44.480 --> 00:18:50.200
Traveling along tiny corridors, they move from pocket to pocket of perfectly refrigerated

00:18:50.200 --> 00:18:55.200
food.

00:18:55.200 --> 00:18:57.680
Snow is a great insulator.

00:18:57.680 --> 00:19:03.740
So down here, the temperature never falls more than a degree or so below zero, and that's

00:19:03.740 --> 00:19:16.560
warm enough for a vole to thrive, even if it lacks the stature of a bison.

00:19:16.560 --> 00:19:22.720
In some years, vole numbers boom, and then they're forced to break cover to search for

00:19:22.720 --> 00:19:52.240
more food.

00:19:52.240 --> 00:20:04.760
The great gray owl is a silent and very skillful hunter, but deep snow can be beyond its reach.

00:20:04.760 --> 00:20:08.920
But such snow is no barrier to one predator.

00:20:08.920 --> 00:20:21.020
The least weasel, a tiny hunter, and the vole's nemesis.

00:20:21.020 --> 00:20:26.880
Its body is exactly the same width as a vole's, so there's nowhere a vole can go that the

00:20:26.880 --> 00:20:29.880
weasel can't follow.

00:20:29.880 --> 00:20:56.500
So yeah election2.

00:20:56.500 --> 00:21:18.940
The weasel's long, slender shape is perfect for hunting in tunnels, but the worst possible

00:21:18.940 --> 00:21:33.300
shape for staying warm, so they need a special way of doing that.

00:21:33.300 --> 00:21:46.740
She plucks the fur from its body, tuft by tuft.

00:21:46.740 --> 00:21:52.740
And now she puts it all together to make a cosy blanket under which to sleep in her den

00:21:52.740 --> 00:22:08.860
below the snow.

00:22:08.860 --> 00:22:12.960
Midwinter in the northern forests.

00:22:12.960 --> 00:22:22.780
The sun is so low that it's twilight at midday.

00:22:22.780 --> 00:22:28.180
Farther north in the high Arctic, the sun has been below the horizon for months, and

00:22:28.180 --> 00:22:53.180
there will be several more before it appears again.

00:22:53.180 --> 00:23:21.020
The

00:23:21.020 --> 00:23:28.020
male polar bear spends winter out on the frozen ocean.

00:23:28.020 --> 00:23:37.140
Few creatures can endure these conditions.

00:23:37.140 --> 00:23:42.260
If he's lucky, he will find a carcass that could provide a little food, possibly that

00:23:42.260 --> 00:23:46.420
of a fox or another polar bear.

00:23:46.420 --> 00:23:54.460
But otherwise, he must live on his reserves.

00:23:54.460 --> 00:24:08.180
This is the time to scrape by, to wait.

00:24:08.180 --> 00:24:33.980
But on lee-side slopes beneath the snow, new lives are beginning.

00:24:33.980 --> 00:24:47.860
The cubs are born blind and tiny, and early birth is easier on the mother, who is barely

00:24:47.860 --> 00:24:56.500
awake.

00:24:56.500 --> 00:25:16.260
Despite her sleepiness, her instinct to nurse is overwhelming.

00:25:16.260 --> 00:25:21.220
The cubs' clucking calls stimulate her to produce milk.

00:25:21.220 --> 00:25:22.980
And what milk?

00:25:22.980 --> 00:25:47.700
It's nine times richer than her own, and enables her to double their weight every few weeks.

00:25:47.700 --> 00:25:52.700
It's over two months since the autumn snows first arrived.

00:25:52.700 --> 00:25:58.340
In two more months, polar bear families will emerge onto the snowy slopes all around the

00:25:58.340 --> 00:25:59.340
Arctic.

00:25:59.340 --> 00:26:28.380
But for now, they lie protected within their icy cocoons.

00:26:28.380 --> 00:26:44.340
Over 100 miles above the Earth, the aurora lights up the sky.

00:26:44.340 --> 00:26:49.740
After traveling millions of miles across space, solar winds attracted by the magnetic pull

00:26:49.740 --> 00:26:55.580
of the poles collide with the Earth's atmosphere.

00:26:55.580 --> 00:27:08.820
Trillions of charged particles dance across the sky.

00:27:08.820 --> 00:27:14.700
Above the Arctic, the aurora borealis, the northern lights.

00:27:14.700 --> 00:27:21.260
In the south, it's the aurora australis, the southern lights, that bring light to Antarctica's

00:27:21.260 --> 00:27:31.340
long winter.

00:27:31.340 --> 00:27:35.260
These spectacular light shows are only a tease.

00:27:35.260 --> 00:27:53.340
So the energy may be, but no warmth that will help the emperor penguins.

00:27:53.340 --> 00:27:59.460
The Arctic winter is brutally cold, but in Antarctica, the darkest months are even more

00:27:59.460 --> 00:28:16.740
savage.

00:28:16.740 --> 00:28:30.260
The male penguins have not eaten for months and have only each other for protection from

00:28:30.260 --> 00:28:33.740
the gales.

00:28:33.740 --> 00:28:38.540
Each has been entrusted with a single precious egg balanced on the top of its feet.

00:28:38.540 --> 00:28:44.740
If the egg were to drop onto the ice, even for a moment, the chicken side would die and

00:28:44.740 --> 00:28:55.340
all this would count for nothing.

00:28:55.340 --> 00:29:03.140
The emperors are not entirely alone.

00:29:03.140 --> 00:29:14.980
The Weddell Sea, the only mammal to remain here throughout the winter.

00:29:14.980 --> 00:29:20.100
They must have breathing holes and so have to constantly scrape away the ice that threatens

00:29:20.100 --> 00:29:31.180
to close them, rasping away fresh buildups with special wide gaped jaws.

00:29:31.180 --> 00:29:39.620
Beneath the ice, they are beyond the reach of the bitter winter winds.

00:29:39.620 --> 00:29:58.700
The sea is minus two degrees centigrade, a warm bath compared to the conditions overhead.

00:29:58.700 --> 00:30:05.620
The roof of ice insulates this world from the wild fluctuations above.

00:30:05.620 --> 00:30:19.300
The temperature down here has barely changed for 25 million years.

00:30:19.300 --> 00:30:23.180
Of course, animals must still be hardy.

00:30:23.180 --> 00:30:28.740
These Borgravinke fish have antifreeze in their blood so they are untroubled swimming

00:30:28.740 --> 00:30:36.420
among the ice crystals.

00:30:36.420 --> 00:30:43.240
Many animals here are remarkably long lived, perhaps because the conditions are so stable.

00:30:43.240 --> 00:30:50.740
Some sponges could be a thousand years old and large enough for a human to hide in.

00:30:50.740 --> 00:30:56.180
There could hardly be a greater contrast to the bleak, windswept world just above.

00:31:20.740 --> 00:31:39.420
But there is a constant danger here.

00:31:39.420 --> 00:31:43.020
Swirling patterns in the water reveal its presence.

00:31:43.020 --> 00:31:49.780
They're made by brine, super concentrated salt water.

00:31:49.780 --> 00:31:55.740
It's a warning.

00:31:55.740 --> 00:32:08.220
New sea ice forming above leaves behind brine that is so extremely salty it sinks rapidly.

00:32:08.220 --> 00:32:14.920
As it descends, the sea water around it freezes instantly and forms a sheath of ice, a brine

00:32:14.920 --> 00:32:28.860
nickel that grows downwards towards the seafloor.

00:32:28.860 --> 00:32:46.260
Winter is reaching down from the cold world above.

00:32:46.260 --> 00:33:13.020
As it touches the seafloor, it kills whatever living thing it contacts by encasing it in

00:33:13.020 --> 00:33:20.860
the water.

00:33:20.860 --> 00:33:26.300
Even in the relative warmth of the water, the lethal cold of winter threatens life on

00:33:26.300 --> 00:33:38.260
the seafloor.

00:33:38.260 --> 00:33:43.340
Winter, more constant attack, rises from below.

00:33:43.340 --> 00:33:51.900
Pressure in the depths keeps water liquid even though its temperature is far from freezing.

00:33:51.900 --> 00:33:57.420
Currents bring up this colder water and it turns to ice, covering everything that can't

00:33:57.420 --> 00:34:20.940
move away from it.

00:34:20.940 --> 00:34:49.620
And then the ice, being lighter than water, begins to float, lifting away anything attached.

00:34:49.620 --> 00:35:08.540
And some of the faster moving animals are caught.

00:35:08.540 --> 00:35:23.500
So the ice cleanses the seafloor and screws the ceiling above with remnants of life from

00:35:23.500 --> 00:35:25.500
below.

00:35:25.500 --> 00:35:33.300
A sign that winter is nearly over.

00:35:33.300 --> 00:35:45.120
The songs of male Weddell seals challenging their rivals to battle.

00:35:45.120 --> 00:36:00.820
The male seal's calls can be heard over 15 miles away.

00:36:00.820 --> 00:36:05.940
He aims to control the best breathing holes, for they will determine his mating rights

00:36:05.940 --> 00:36:14.900
in spring.

00:36:14.900 --> 00:36:19.420
His calls create powerful shock waves in the water.

00:36:19.420 --> 00:36:31.900
Their threats.

00:36:31.900 --> 00:36:47.140
This hole has already been claimed and the owner will not surrender it willingly.

00:36:47.140 --> 00:36:49.780
No change this time.

00:36:49.780 --> 00:37:01.460
The challenger will need to find a hole with a weaker owner.

00:37:01.460 --> 00:37:25.340
The battles continue until the females arrive and that time is now close.

00:37:25.340 --> 00:37:40.460
The sun returns to Antarctica.

00:37:40.460 --> 00:38:06.020
The longest night on Earth has ended and winter begins to give way to spring.

00:38:06.020 --> 00:38:22.380
Female emperor penguins, after four months feeding at sea, they're returning sleek and

00:38:22.380 --> 00:38:36.940
fat.

00:38:36.940 --> 00:39:00.420
Penguins, it seems, can fly after all.

00:39:00.420 --> 00:39:27.940
But a winter at sea has left them a little out of practice.

00:39:27.940 --> 00:39:56.700
There

00:39:56.700 --> 00:39:58.660
is no time to waste.

00:39:58.660 --> 00:40:12.060
Far away, the males are waiting.

00:40:12.060 --> 00:40:17.460
While the females were gone, the sea ice grew and it's now twice the distance from its edge

00:40:17.460 --> 00:40:27.060
back to their colony.

00:40:27.060 --> 00:40:34.060
Seventy miles away, the males are in desperate need of help.

00:40:34.060 --> 00:40:54.940
Despite doing all they can to conserve their energy, many are close to dying from starvation.

00:24:51.920 --> 00:41:56.540
first glimpse of her chick.

00:41:56.540 --> 00:42:05.060
With a reunion made, it's time for the handover.

00:42:05.060 --> 00:42:11.700
The father finds it hard to let go.

00:42:11.700 --> 00:42:16.500
Some gentle persuasion is needed.

00:42:16.500 --> 00:42:22.300
He has endured the most appalling conditions on Earth to ensure the survival of his chick

00:42:22.300 --> 00:42:26.660
and the bond is strong.

00:42:26.660 --> 00:42:44.220
The exchange must be quick as the tiny chick, unprotected, could freeze to death in seconds.

00:42:44.220 --> 00:42:48.300
A task that began in autumn has been completed.

00:42:48.300 --> 00:42:53.900
Despite the huge odds against it, the precious chick has survived the winter and is now with

00:42:53.900 --> 00:42:55.220
its mother.

00:42:55.220 --> 00:43:07.260
And she has food.

00:43:07.260 --> 00:43:11.700
The chick's first fresh meal.

00:43:11.700 --> 00:43:17.620
Her mission is complete.

00:43:17.620 --> 00:43:23.420
But for other mothers, the journey ends in disappointment.

00:43:23.420 --> 00:43:31.780
Their chicks have not survived.

00:43:31.780 --> 00:43:35.340
The females need to nurture remain strong.

00:43:35.340 --> 00:43:48.860
Any chick that strays from its parent is at grave risk of being kidnapped.

00:43:48.860 --> 00:44:17.260
The

00:44:17.260 --> 00:44:40.820
chaos may be a consequence of frustrated parental urge, but the outcome can be tragic.

00:44:40.820 --> 00:44:48.740
For those emperor penguins that survive, the worst is over for this year.

00:44:48.740 --> 00:45:04.340
There will soon be abundant food for everyone.

00:45:04.340 --> 00:45:08.900
The emperors have taken on the polar winter and won.

00:45:08.900 --> 00:45:12.580
The gamble has paid off.

00:45:12.580 --> 00:45:14.700
All other animals escaped.

00:45:14.700 --> 00:45:17.820
Only they remain to raise their young.

00:45:17.820 --> 00:45:32.100
And now it is they who will benefit most from the rich southern spring.

00:45:32.100 --> 00:45:36.700
Another arrival is the certain confirmation that string is here.

00:45:36.700 --> 00:45:40.780
Only the damens, having spent winter at sea, have come back.

00:45:40.780 --> 00:45:53.420
But they have not even mated yet, let alone laid their eggs.

00:45:53.420 --> 00:46:14.860
Now at last, the male emperors can return to where they are most at home.

00:46:14.860 --> 00:46:19.300
One season ends and another begins.

00:46:19.300 --> 00:46:25.420
The penguins will soon be joined by migrants and the far south will bustle with life for

00:46:25.420 --> 00:46:37.500
a few frantic months.

00:46:37.500 --> 00:46:44.780
But there are only a special few, north and south, who can survive winter at the farthest

00:46:44.780 --> 00:46:49.820
ends of our planet.

00:47:14.780 --> 00:47:27.060
Next time, Frozen Planet is with the people of the polar regions.

00:47:27.060 --> 00:47:55.420
From traditional ways of survival to the very frontiers of science.

00:47:55.420 --> 00:48:00.020
More than any other season in the poles, winter was to pose the greatest challenge to the

00:48:00.020 --> 00:48:02.700
Frozen Planet crew.

00:48:02.700 --> 00:48:08.220
The team endured winds of over 100 miles an hour and temperatures as low as minus 50 degrees

00:48:08.220 --> 00:48:10.500
centigrade.

00:48:10.500 --> 00:48:20.740
Their boats were trapped in sea ice for days, while bears trapped others indoors.

00:48:20.740 --> 00:48:26.940
Some of the coldest conditions experienced were near the Arctic Circle in northern Canada.

00:48:26.940 --> 00:48:32.980
In winter, temperatures dropped to minus 40 degrees and stayed there.

00:48:32.980 --> 00:48:37.720
On the edge of the vast tiger forest, the team hoped to film one of the most remarkable

00:48:37.720 --> 00:48:42.120
predator-prey relationships on the planet.

00:48:42.120 --> 00:48:46.000
What they experienced was the struggle against the elements.

00:48:46.000 --> 00:49:01.380
What they witnessed was a more profound struggle for life and death.

00:49:01.380 --> 00:49:08.120
Wood Buffalo National Park covers 28,000 square miles, the size of Denmark.

00:49:08.120 --> 00:49:12.660
One of the few cameramen to have filmed wolves and bison here is Jeff Turner.

00:49:12.660 --> 00:49:18.420
I first tried to film bison and wolves in this national park 15 years ago.

00:49:18.420 --> 00:49:22.540
And getting around on the ground is incredibly difficult at any time of the year, but in

00:49:22.540 --> 00:49:24.580
the winter, it's tough.

00:49:24.580 --> 00:49:28.460
We quickly realized that the only way we were going to get anything here was we had to get

00:49:28.460 --> 00:49:29.460
up in the air.

00:49:29.460 --> 00:49:37.100
The Frozen Planet team had never attempted aerial filming in such low temperatures.

00:49:37.100 --> 00:49:42.140
The first challenge for director Chatham Hunter was protecting the sensitive aerial camera

00:49:42.140 --> 00:49:54.180
and minus 40 electrical cables short-circuit like fireworks.

00:49:54.180 --> 00:49:59.100
Aerial cameraman Michael Kellum comes from sunny California and has never experienced

00:49:59.100 --> 00:50:11.060
temperatures so low.

00:50:11.060 --> 00:50:15.140
Having delicate controls while wearing gloves is not easy.

00:50:15.140 --> 00:50:19.740
The computer and joystick are needed to control the aerial camera attached to the outside

00:50:19.740 --> 00:50:22.740
of the helicopter.

00:50:22.740 --> 00:50:29.420
Michael would have to choose between dexterity or warmth.

00:50:29.420 --> 00:50:33.620
With their camera mounted on the nose, the aerial team could now fly hundreds of miles

00:50:33.620 --> 00:50:36.780
in search of the animals.

00:50:36.780 --> 00:50:54.500
Jeff meanwhile is still on foot scouting the other side of the national park.

00:50:54.500 --> 00:50:59.140
From 3,000 feet up, Chatham catches a glimpse of the elusive wolf pack.

00:50:59.140 --> 00:51:17.100
The wolf pack turns out to number an extraordinary 25 individuals, one of the largest ever filmed.

00:51:17.100 --> 00:51:19.780
And it looks like they're already on the hunt.

00:51:19.780 --> 00:51:24.340
The helicopter allows the team to shadow the wolf pack without disturbing them.

00:51:24.340 --> 00:51:45.220
The pack are moving in on their prey and the aerial team are perfectly placed.

00:51:45.220 --> 00:51:46.900
The chase is on.

00:51:46.900 --> 00:51:51.060
Extreme concentration is now needed both from the pilot and the cameraman to keep the shot

00:51:51.060 --> 00:51:52.060
smooth.

00:51:52.060 --> 00:52:16.860
The wolf pack have picked out a young bison.

00:52:16.860 --> 00:52:27.140
As Michael struggles to hold the shot steady, no one is prepared for what came next.

00:52:27.140 --> 00:52:39.220
A one ton bull charges through, taking out both the wolves and the young bison.

00:52:39.220 --> 00:52:45.720
The team have filmed extraordinary new behaviour from the air, but Jeff was nowhere nearby.

00:52:45.720 --> 00:52:48.860
To complete the sequence they will need to work together.

00:52:48.860 --> 00:52:53.820
Only by using the helicopter to position Jeff will they be able to get both air and ground

00:52:53.820 --> 00:53:04.180
coverage.

00:53:04.180 --> 00:53:10.660
This pack will now feast for days, so the crew must move on.

00:53:10.660 --> 00:53:15.540
Jeff takes the team deeper into the wilderness in search of a new wolf pack.

00:53:15.540 --> 00:53:20.420
With everyone on board there are more eyes for spotting.

00:53:20.420 --> 00:53:28.300
Bison on the run can only mean one thing, wolves, but this time only a pair.

00:53:28.300 --> 00:53:33.460
Jeff must anticipate the animals every move and direct the helicopter to where he needs

00:53:33.460 --> 00:53:38.340
to be dropped off.

00:53:38.340 --> 00:53:41.740
Had to figure out exactly where the buffalo were going to run and try to get there ahead

00:53:41.740 --> 00:53:42.740
of them.

00:53:42.740 --> 00:53:47.020
If I got dropped off in the wrong position we would blow it.

00:53:47.020 --> 00:53:59.780
There wasn't going to be a second chance.

00:53:59.780 --> 00:54:04.740
That moment when you get out of the helicopter and it leaves you behind, you feel an incredible

00:54:04.740 --> 00:54:05.740
sense of isolation.

00:54:05.740 --> 00:54:32.100
When I saw the herd coming around the corner I knew we'd picked the right spot.

00:54:32.100 --> 00:54:33.980
I was right in front of them.

00:54:33.980 --> 00:54:41.020
When the buffalo are running right towards you it definitely does get your heart pounding.

00:54:41.020 --> 00:54:44.180
The bison have poor eyesight and can't see Jeff.

00:54:44.180 --> 00:54:47.100
It's now a test of nerves.

00:54:47.100 --> 00:54:51.700
Weighing one ton and running at 40 miles an hour, one wrong step and the bison could kill

00:54:51.700 --> 00:54:52.700
Jeff.

00:54:52.700 --> 00:54:58.780
At the last second they spot him and three peel away to the other side.

00:54:58.780 --> 00:55:18.100
At the back of the herd the wolves have closed in.

00:55:18.100 --> 00:55:22.860
What followed was one of the most emotional and powerful scenes the Frozen Planet team

00:55:22.860 --> 00:55:26.100
were ever to film.

00:55:26.100 --> 00:55:28.820
Jeff has picked his position perfectly.

00:55:28.820 --> 00:55:35.660
He is now only 50 meters from the animals and able to film a truly epic battle.

00:55:35.660 --> 00:55:39.580
I had never been so close to a wolf and bison battle.

00:55:39.580 --> 00:55:44.620
I could hear them breathing and the power of the buffalo.

00:55:44.620 --> 00:55:48.140
The way he just threw this wolf around like it was a ragdoll.

00:55:48.140 --> 00:55:55.500
I couldn't believe the beating this wolf was taking.

00:55:55.500 --> 00:56:02.180
The struggles that we'd had with the winter and the cold and the snow just felt so insignificant

00:56:02.180 --> 00:56:08.940
compared to these two animals that were struggling for their very lives.

00:56:08.940 --> 00:56:15.780
For over an hour I watched this wolf and bison battle each other to a standstill.

00:56:15.780 --> 00:56:26.260
They were both unbelievably exhausted.

00:56:26.260 --> 00:56:30.620
Watching these two animals engaged in this massive life and death struggle was one of

00:56:30.620 --> 00:56:36.220
the most powerful things I'd ever witnessed in the wild.

00:56:36.220 --> 00:56:40.060
As a filmmaker you're definitely torn watching something like this.

00:56:40.060 --> 00:56:44.660
You know you need to keep filming and you know this is a natural event but it's also

00:56:44.660 --> 00:56:54.180
incredibly sad to know that one of these two magnificent animals isn't going to make it.

00:56:54.180 --> 00:56:57.300
The bison is fatally wounded.

00:56:57.300 --> 00:57:01.680
The battle is over.

00:57:01.680 --> 00:57:08.180
The team have managed to capture a remarkable hunt from both ground and air.

00:57:08.180 --> 00:57:14.860
All of them have been humbled by witnessing an extraordinary winter struggle for survival.
